rs-key devshell
  rustc:    rustc 1.96.0 (ac68faa20 2026-05-25)
  target:   thumbv8m.main-none-eabihf
  picotool: picotool v2.2.0-a4 (Darwin, Clang-21.1.8, Release)

Build:  cargo build --release -p firmware   # pick the target crate
  (or:  nix build .#firmware                 # hermetic → result/firmware.uf2)
PT:     scripts/pt.sh target/thumbv8m.main-none-eabihf/release/firmware fw-pt.elf   # fence the KV store
UF2:    picotool uf2 convert fw-pt.elf -t elf firmware.uf2
Flash:  hold BOOTSEL, plug in the RP2350, drag firmware.uf2 to the RP2350 drive
Check:  ./scripts/check.sh        # fmt + clippy + test + audit + deny + gitleaks
Fuzz:   nix develop .#fuzz -c cargo fuzz run <target>
CLI:    rsk status | rsk backup … | rsk secure-boot … | rsk otp … (rsk --help)
TUI:    rsk-tui                    # live device dashboard
Docs:   ./scripts/docs.sh serve    # preview the docs site (build|check)
reader: RS-Key RS-Key Security Key

Requesting the presence-gated reboot to BOOTSEL — touch the device…
  SELECT vendor: 9000

Dumped 532480 bytes of SRAM to sram.bin
  control: 4096 B of .text matches the ELF ✓ (picoboot reads work, and this ELF is the flashed image)
  control: 0/18 immutable .data symbols byte-exact, 2943/7836 .data bytes match the ELF

RESULT: flash reads back, SRAM does not.
  whole dump: 532480 B, 0 non-zero, 1 distinct
  read-back probe: a pattern written to 0x20010000 reads straight back ✓

SETTLED: main SRAM does not survive the drop to BOOTSEL — every byte of the dump is zero, not just the control window. picoboot serves SRAM faithfully, so those zeros are the memory itself and nothing is recoverable from RAM this way. Note what it is NOT evidence of: the platform cleared it, not worker::reboot's scrub, and the result belongs to this silicon revision and boot configuration.
